i i want to jump back in time because we  have been taking for granted the  existence of  instant photography so we're looking  here this seems like a very odd image  of a famous italian sculpture of  david it's one of four it's taken in  florence and the image was taken  by an art historian by the name of  clarence  kennedy kennedy was a pioneering  figure in the photography of sculpture  this exhibit subtitle is the  intersection of art and technology  and i think it's um encapsulated best by  this early relationship between clarence  kennedy who came  to meet edwin land and george wainwright  kennedy met these two young  men and said look i am desperately  trying to aid my students  to better get a feeling  for sculpture and art but with sculpture  you know the essence is being able to  walk around them and  i wanted to find a new technology  uh using some sort of 3d imaging and  that conversation stimulated edwin land  tremendously and they suggested that he  try a technique of  taking a stereoscope set of photographs  one for your  left eye one for your right eye  projecting them carefully on a screen  and then they provided some polarizing  lenses and all of a sudden there was  a 3d image and  this technology was one that kennedy  and land continued to work on throughout  the 1930s and it led to  what you're seeing here in this image  it's called a vector graph  and that was a technique  of taking two negative images  dipping them into a  chemical solution for about four or five  minutes  and then pressing them together onto a  single base  but if you picked up the glasses that  are in that bucket  on to the left of the vector graph here  of david and put them on  all of a sudden you would now see the  sculpture  uh rendered in 3d  that was very magical and very exciting  even in the 60s and 70s and 80s people  felt that the vector graph  was the preeminent form of 3d  still imaging but i tell this story here  in some detail  because the famous origin story  of polaroid is that in december of 1943  land takes his daughter jennifer for a  walk  and they take some photographs and  jennifer  being three or four years old at the  time  says at the end of their walk why can't  i see the pictures now  and the reason he could think it was  possible was  all the work that he had done on the  vector graph technology  i think our origin story of polaroid  actually is predates jennifer's question  and goes back to that relationship with  clarence kennedy  you
 

